#limitation(title: [Pacing Evidence Base])[
While pacing is widely recommended by ME/CFS specialists and patient organizations, and large patient surveys consistently rank it as the most helpful management strategy, high-quality randomized controlled trial evidence is limited. Most supporting evidence comes from observational studies, patient surveys, and clinical experience. The optimal pacing strategy likely varies by patient severity and subtype.

*Large-scale patient-reported support.* In the largest treatment-outcome survey to date (Eckey et al.\ 2025, $n = 3{,}925$), pacing received the highest Net Assessment Score of any treatment (75.2%) and — uniquely among the treatments evaluated — was reported effective across *all four* symptom clusters and across symptom domains (fatigue 82.7%, PEM 62.6%, brain fog 71.2%, POTS 45.5%) @Eckey2025PatientReported. This strengthens the survey-based foundation of the pacing recommendation, though it remains patient-reported and unblinded rather than RCT-level evidence. (Severity: all levels, across severity strata.)

*Contested trial evidence.* Randomised trials of pacing-as-intervention are weaker than the theoretical energy-envelope case. A trial of activity-pacing self-management ($n = 33$) found performance and participation improved in *both* the pacing and the relaxation arms ($p = .03$), with pacing favoured only on the satisfaction subscale (effect size 0.74) (@Kos2015ActivityPacing). In a comparison of two education interventions ($n = 48$), pacing education was the weaker arm against pain-physiology education (@Meeus2010PainPhysiologyEducation). These sit alongside the positive observational and survey evidence above: pacing has strong patient-reported support and a coherent mechanistic rationale, but the controlled-trial demonstration of a specific pacing effect on core outcomes is partial or null. One candidate explanation for the both-arms-improve pattern is that the shared active ingredient is scheduled, unbroken rest time itself — an opportunity for recovery and slow-wave-coupled clearance — rather than the activity-rationing rationale that distinguishes pacing from relaxation. *Falsifiable prediction:* a trial matching total rest time across pacing and control arms should find no between-group difference on fatigue or PEM; this rest-dose account is falsified if pacing outperforms a rest-matched control on core endpoints.

*Consequence:* pacing remains the most patient-endorsed management strategy, but the trial evidence for a specific pacing effect is thin; recommending it rests on patient-reported and mechanistic grounds rather than randomised demonstration.
]
